from jsonb_to_recordset($json$[{"id":"che-ek-149","chapter_id":"chemistry-electrochemistry-and-chemical-kinetics","subject_id":"chemistry","stem":"For Zn–Cu with $E^\\circ = 1.10\\ \\mathrm{V}$ and $n = 2$, $\\Delta G^\\circ$ is nearest ($F = 96500\\ \\mathrm{C\\ mol^{-1}}$)","options":["$-212\\ \\mathrm{kJ\\ mol^{-1}}$","$-106\\ \\mathrm{kJ\\ mol^{-1}}$","$+212\\ \\mathrm{kJ\\ mol^{-1}}$","$-1.10\\ \\mathrm{kJ\\ mol^{-1}}$"],"answer_index":0,"explanation":"$\\Delta G^\\circ = -2 \\times 96500 \\times 1.10 = -212300\\ \\mathrm{J\\ mol^{-1}} \\approx -212\\ \\mathrm{kJ\\ mol^{-1}}$.","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"hard","concept_id":"cells-emf","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false},{"id":"che-ek-150","chapter_id":"chemistry-electrochemistry-and-chemical-kinetics","subject_id":"chemistry","stem":"Couples: $\\mathrm{Ni^{2+}/Ni} = -0.25\\ \\mathrm{V}$, $\\mathrm{Ag^+/Ag} = +0.80\\ \\mathrm{V}$. The spontaneous standard cell and its $E^\\circ$ are","options":["Ni anode, Ag cathode; $1.05\\ \\mathrm{V}$","Ag anode, Ni cathode; $1.05\\ \\mathrm{V}$","Ni anode, Ag cathode; $0.55\\ \\mathrm{V}$","Ag anode, Ni cathode; $0.55\\ \\mathrm{V}$"],"answer_index":0,"explanation":"Ni is the better reductant, so it is the anode. $E^\\circ = 0.80 - (-0.25) = 1.05\\ \\mathrm{V}$.","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"hard","concept_id":"cells-emf","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false},{"id":"che-ek-151","chapter_id":"chemistry-electrochemistry-and-chemical-kinetics","subject_id":"chemistry","stem":"The Nernst equation for a cell reaction with reaction quotient $Q$ is","options":["$E = E^\\circ - (RT/nF)\\ln Q$","$E = E^\\circ + (RT/nF)\\ln Q$","$E = E^\\circ - nF\\ln Q$","$E = (RT/nF)\\ln Q$ only"],"answer_index":0,"explanation":"The correction term subtracts a quantity that grows with $Q$, so the cell voltage falls as products accumulate.","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"easy","concept_id":"nernst","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false},{"id":"che-ek-152","chapter_id":"chemistry-electrochemistry-and-chemical-kinetics","subject_id":"chemistry","stem":"At $298\\ \\mathrm{K}$ the Nernst factor $2.303RT/F$ is close to","options":["$0.059\\ \\mathrm{V}$","$0.059\\ \\mathrm{mV}$","$8.314\\ \\mathrm{V}$","$96500\\ \\mathrm{V}$"],"answer_index":0,"explanation":"Intermediate problems therefore write $E = E^\\circ - (0.059/n)\\log Q$ at 25 °C.","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"easy","concept_id":"nernst","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false},{"id":"che-ek-153","chapter_id":"chemistry-electrochemistry-and-chemical-kinetics","subject_id":"chemistry","stem":"When every species in the cell reaction is in its standard state, $Q = 1$ and therefore","options":["$E = E^\\circ$","$E = 0$ always","$E = 0.059\\ \\mathrm{V}$","$E = -E^\\circ$"],"answer_index":0,"explanation":"The log term vanishes at $Q = 1$, recovering the standard EMF.","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"easy","concept_id":"nernst","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false},{"id":"che-ek-154","chapter_id":"chemistry-electrochemistry-and-chemical-kinetics","subject_id":"chemistry","stem":"A concentration cell that uses the same couple on both sides has","options":["$E^\\circ = 0$, but a non-zero $E$ if the two concentrations differ","$E^\\circ$ equal to twice a single-electrode potential","no possible EMF under any condition","$E$ independent of both concentrations"],"answer_index":0,"explanation":"The two standard potentials cancel. The remaining voltage comes only from the concentration (activity) ratio.","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"easy","concept_id":"nernst","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false},{"id":"che-ek-155","chapter_id":"chemistry-electrochemistry-and-chemical-kinetics","subject_id":"chemistry","stem":"As the reaction quotient $Q$ of a galvanic cell increases, the cell EMF generally","options":["decreases","increases without limit","jumps discontinuously to $E^\\circ$","becomes independent of $n$"],"answer_index":0,"explanation":"$E = E^\\circ - (RT/nF)\\ln Q$, so a larger $Q$ reduces $E$. At equilibrium $Q = K$ and $E = 0$.","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"easy","concept_id":"nernst","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false},{"id":"che-ek-156","chapter_id":"chemistry-electrochemistry-and-chemical-kinetics","subject_id":"chemistry","stem":"For the half-cell $\\mathrm{M}^{n+} + n\\,e^- \\rightarrow \\mathrm{M}(s)$, the Nernst expression at 298 K is","options":["$E = E^\\circ + (0.059/n)\\log[\\mathrm{M}^{n+}]$","$E = E^\\circ - (0.059/n)\\log[\\mathrm{M}^{n+}]$","$E = E^\\circ + 0.059\\,n\\,[\\mathrm{M}^{n+}]$","$E = (0.059/n)/[\\mathrm{M}^{n+}]$"],"answer_index":0,"explanation":"Solid metal has activity 1, so $Q = 1/[\\mathrm{M}^{n+}]$ and the sign of the log term flips.","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"easy","concept_id":"nernst","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false},{"id":"che-ek-157","chapter_id":"chemistry-electrochemistry-and-chemical-kinetics","subject_id":"chemistry","stem":"The integer $n$ that appears in the Nernst equation is the","options":["number of moles of electrons transferred in the balanced cell reaction","number of ions in the salt bridge","atomic number of the anode metal","order of the electrode reaction"],"answer_index":0,"explanation":"$n$ comes from the balanced redox equation (for Daniell, $n = 2$).","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"easy","concept_id":"nernst","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false},{"id":"che-ek-158","chapter_id":"chemistry-electrochemistry-and-chemical-kinetics","subject_id":"chemistry","stem":"When a galvanic cell has run down to equilibrium, the Nernst voltage satisfies","options":["$E = 0$ and $Q = K$","$E = E^\\circ$ and $Q = 0$","$E = \\infty$","$E = -nF$"],"answer_index":0,"explanation":"No further driving force remains ($E = 0$), which forces $Q$ to equal the equilibrium constant $K$.","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"easy","concept_id":"nernst","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false},{"id":"che-ek-159","chapter_id":"chemistry-electrochemistry-and-chemical-kinetics","subject_id":"chemistry","stem":"The potential of a hydrogen electrode at 298 K, $p_{\\mathrm{H_2}} = 1\\ \\mathrm{bar}$, varies with pH as","options":["$E = -0.059\\,\\mathrm{pH}$","$E = +0.059\\,\\mathrm{pH}$","$E = 0.059/\\mathrm{pH}$","$E = 1.23 - 0.059\\,\\mathrm{pH}$"],"answer_index":0,"explanation":"$E = 0.059\\log[\\mathrm{H}^+] = -0.059\\,\\mathrm{pH}$ when hydrogen is at unit pressure.","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"easy","concept_id":"nernst","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false},{"id":"che-ek-160","chapter_id":"chemistry-electrochemistry-and-chemical-kinetics","subject_id":"chemistry","stem":"Raising the concentration of a product ion in the cell reaction, other terms fixed, will","options":["lower the cell EMF","raise $E^\\circ$","leave both $E$ and $Q$ unchanged","reverse the definition of the anode"],"answer_index":0,"explanation":"Products appear in the numerator of $Q$. A larger $Q$ makes $(RT/nF)\\ln Q$ larger and $E$ smaller.","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"easy","concept_id":"nernst","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false}]$json$::jsonb) as x(
